# What belongs in the source archive (the .tar.gz).
#
# Without this file the archive carried the package and the test
# CODE, but not: the ArcGIS toolbox, the Stata door, the test
# fixtures those tests read, or CITATION.cff. So the tests it
# shipped could not run, and an academic package went out without
# its citation. Long-standing (1.17.3 on PyPI has the same gap);
# fixed in 1.18.0, where it matters more - the toolbox and the
# package became two halves of one thing and belong in one archive.

include CITATION.cff
include MANUAL.md
include MANUAL_BEGINNER.md
include MANUAL_TOPICS.md
include BACKLOG.md

# The doors that pip cannot deliver: a user installs the package,
# then copies these onto the machine by hand.
graft arcgis
graft stata
graft qgis

# Fixtures the shipped tests actually read. (The datasets the Book
# uses moved into equipop/data in 1.19.0 and ride in the wheel.)
graft tests/data

# ...and the helper modules those tests IMPORT. setuptools ships
# files named test*.py by an old default and nothing else beside
# them, so tests/qgis_stub.py - the simulated PyQGIS, present since
# 1.20.0 - had never entered a single archive. Every published
# archive from 1.20.0 to 1.28.0 therefore cannot even COLLECT its
# own test suite: `No module named 'qgis_stub'`. Found in 1.29.0 by
# the check the conventions demand and which had evidently not been
# run since 1.19.0 - unpack the .tar.gz into an empty directory and
# run the whole suite from inside it. Same class of miss as the one
# this file was written for.
include tests/*.py

# Worked examples, and the Book's sources.
graft examples
graft docs

# Tools that must travel with the code they check. stub_audit.py
# is run in a LIVE QGIS each release (BACKLOG 80) - it is the
# only check that can see the simulator flattering itself, so an
# archive without it ships the problem and not the detector.
# Missed on the first build of 1.29.1 and caught by the archive
# check, which is the second time that check has earned itself.
graft tools

# Never: build leftovers, the Book's compiled output, its node
# packages, or its figures - build.sh regenerates every figure from
# examples/cookbook_0*.py, and they were 4.2 MB of a 4.8 MB archive.
prune docs/book/node_modules
prune docs/book/figs
global-exclude *.docx
global-exclude *.pyc
global-exclude __pycache__/*
